WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:06.400
Ben Wattenberg: Hello, I’m Ben Wattenberg.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.610
As you may have noticed, men look different
from women.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.280
But do men and women think differently?

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:18.440
If so, could these differences be hardwired
in our genes and in our brains?

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:19.680
What would that mean for women?

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.340
What would that mean for men?

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:27.250
Joining us to sort through the conflict and
the consensus are Deborah Tannen, professor

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:31.980
of linguistics at Georgetown University and
author of “You Just Don’t Understand:

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:38.660
Women and Men in Conversation” and “Talking
from 9 to 5”; Robert Wright of The New Republic

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.410
magazine and author of “The Moral Animal:
Why We Are the Way We Are: The New Science

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:48.980
of Evolutionary Psychology”; Lawrence Hedges,
professor of social science and education

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:54.390
at the University of Chicago and coauthor
of a major new study on gender differences;

00:00:54.390 --> 00:01:00.040
and Christina Sommers, professor of philosophy
at Clark University and author of “Who Stole

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.040
Feminism?

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.100
How Women Have Betrayed Women.”

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:07.850
The topic before this house is: How different
are men and women?

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:09.500
This week on “Think Tank.”

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:28.550
“Boys are made of frogs and snails and puppy
dog tails.”

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:33.300
And girls — “girls are made of sugar and
spice and all that’s nice.”

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.450
You know, for more than a quarter of a century,
the women’s movement has been chipping away

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:40.420
at stereotypes like those.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:45.630
In America now, the economic, political and
professional imbalances between men and women

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:53.159
are closing fast, but the evidence behind
some of those stereotypes is mounting.

00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:58.520
Recent studies reveal that men and women use
different parts of their brains for language.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:03.260
Girls are nearly twice as good as boys on
many word choice tests, and girls are far

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:07.590
more likely to be top scorers on writing tests.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:13.060
Boys are seven times more likely than girls
to score in the top 5 percent on science tests

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:17.010
and twice as likely to score higher on math
tests.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:18.769
What about the mating game?

00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:24.790
According to a survey of 37 cultures, men
tend to value physical attractiveness and

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:30.629
youth when choosing a partner, while women
are more concerned about status, ambition,

00:02:30.629 --> 00:02:32.189
and resources.

00:02:32.189 --> 00:02:35.420
What happens when social barriers to women
fall?

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:42.019
Since 1960, the share of women receiving medical
degrees has increased sevenfold, while the

00:02:42.019 --> 00:02:45.870
number receiving law degrees has jumped 17-fold.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:52.849
Today the majority of college students are
women, who on the average receive better grades

00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:54.909
than their male counterparts.

00:02:54.909 --> 00:03:04.669
A few months ago, Speaker Newt Gingrich declared
that men are biologically driven to hunt and

00:03:04.669 --> 00:03:11.809
kill giraffes and that women were biologically
unsuited to fighting in the trenches.

00:03:11.809 --> 00:03:15.540
Let’s just go around the room once, starting
with you, Deborah Tannen.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.780
How different are men and women?

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:22.029
Deborah Tannen: The differences that I’m
interested in are differences in ways of speaking,

00:03:22.029 --> 00:03:25.140
ways of using language in everyday life.

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:28.249
There seems to be a tendency — and all of
these are just tendencies; they’re never

00:03:28.249 --> 00:03:35.389
absolute differences — tendency for women
to focus more on the connection versus distance

00:03:35.389 --> 00:03:39.870
dimension, men to be somewhat more attuned
to whether a way of speaking is going to put

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:42.049
them in a one-down position.

00:03:42.049 --> 00:03:46.840
And many other differences we can talk about
— ways of valuing the role of conversation

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:52.430
in a relationship, ways of using language
to accomplish your goals in everyday interaction.

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:57.480
Ben Wattenberg: Larry Hedges, you just authored
— coauthored a new study on this matter.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:01.799
Lawrence Hedges: Yes, and the differences
that we looked at were differences in intellectual

00:04:01.799 --> 00:04:02.939
functioning.

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:08.480
And there the short summary is that, on the
average, men and women are not that different

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:14.449
in their intellectual functioning, with a
few very stereotyped exceptions.

00:04:14.449 --> 00:04:18.480
But in spite of the fact that they aren’t
that different on the average, men tend to

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.639
outnumber women substantially at both the
top end of the distribution — that is, among

00:04:22.639 --> 00:04:27.460
very talented individuals — and also at
the bottom end, among the anti-talented individuals,

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:28.479
if you like.

00:04:28.479 --> 00:04:29.490
Ben Wattenberg: Okay.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:30.490
Bob Wright.

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.810
Robert Wright: Well, from the standpoint of
evolutionary psychology, I guess the sex difference

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:39.889
that most certainly has a genetic basis is
in the realm of sex or romance.

00:04:39.889 --> 00:04:44.199
For example, men are less discriminating about
sex partners, in some sense more polygamous,

00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:45.580
on average at least, than women.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:53.199
But the same logic that leads to that conclusion
does extend to the question of aggressiveness,

00:04:53.199 --> 00:04:58.319
although I don’t buy Newt Gingrich’s explanation
of exactly why that is in evolutionary terms.

00:04:58.319 --> 00:05:01.970
Ben Wattenberg: You are permitted by your
magazine to agree with Newt Gingrich periodically,

00:05:01.970 --> 00:05:02.970
right?

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.039
Robert Wright: This was the one — this fulfills
my yearly quota.

00:05:05.039 --> 00:05:06.770
Ben Wattenberg: Okay, all right, great.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:07.879
All right.

00:05:07.879 --> 00:05:13.370
Robert Wright: And this issue of aggression
may in turn extend into realms beyond physical

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:18.100
combat, so that men — for example, Deborah’s
work suggests that they’re more combative

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.100
in conversation.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.100
Ben Wattenberg: Who, us?

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:21.100
Robert Wright: Exactly.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:23.039
Ben Wattenberg: Okay, Christina Sommers.

00:05:23.039 --> 00:05:24.570
Christina Sommers: Yes.

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:28.220
I think the differences can be exaggerated.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:31.130
I do not think that women think differently
from men.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:36.130
I completely disagree with my colleagues in
feminist philosophy, who believe there is

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:39.150
a special epistemology, a female logic.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:40.889
This seems to me to be nonsense.

00:05:40.889 --> 00:05:47.990
However, I do think there are rather strong
differences in preferences between men and

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:54.870
women, romantically speaking, and even in
the areas — in employment, women gravitate

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:57.820
towards jobs that seem to engage them more
emotionally.

00:05:57.820 --> 00:06:03.449
You’re going to always have probably more
women as kindergarten teachers than helicopter

00:06:03.449 --> 00:06:05.330
repair, you know, servicemen.

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:11.850
Deborah Tannen: I often wonder why we are
so obsessed with picking apart exactly how

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:13.990
much is social and exactly how much is genetic.

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:15.290
Ben Wattenberg: Well, because —

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:17.009
Deborah Tannen: It must be some combination
of both.

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:21.120
Ben Wattenberg: Yeah, right, but to answer
your question as to why we are so obsessed

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:26.289
about it, because starting in the 1960s, a
very prominent group of women said there really

00:06:26.289 --> 00:06:31.939
were no differences, these were all socially
imposed upon women, and they were harming

00:06:31.939 --> 00:06:32.939
women.

00:06:32.939 --> 00:06:34.550
So I mean, I think that’s the answer why
we’re in this argument.

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:37.500
Deborah Tannen: Well, and that in itself is
kind of interesting.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.139
I frequently am asked after I speak, “Are
these differences you described biological

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:43.139
or cultural?”

00:06:43.139 --> 00:06:47.039
And I often find that the people who ask me
think they know.

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:50.639
They either are convinced it’s all biological
or they’re convinced it’s all cultural,

00:06:50.639 --> 00:06:53.030
and they tend to pattern by gender.

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:57.320
It’s usually the women who feel it’s all
cultural and the men who ask me and who have

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:00.800
already decided that they think it’s all
biological.

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:04.080
That in itself, to me, is an interesting cultural
phenomenon.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:09.330
I suspect that the women are thinking, if
we can say that there are really no differences,

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:13.639
it’s all cultural, then we can change everything
we don’t like about the world tomorrow.

00:07:13.639 --> 00:07:18.949
And on the other side, the feeling may be,
if it’s all biological, then if you women

00:07:18.949 --> 00:07:21.630
are in a subordinate position in the world,
it’s your own fault.

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:24.589
We can’t do anything about it, and don’t
blame me.

00:07:24.589 --> 00:07:28.139
And I think, in fact, neither of those is
true.

00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:32.969
Cultural training is very hard to change,
and biological training is very possible to

00:07:32.969 --> 00:07:33.969
change.

00:07:33.969 --> 00:07:36.499
I mean, what’s more human than to go against
our biological givens?

00:07:36.499 --> 00:07:40.550
Ben Wattenberg: Bob Wright, where does the
evolutionary psychologist come out on this?

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:45.930
Robert Wright: Well, certainly not with the
view that genes are destiny.

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:51.280
But on the other hand, with the view that
there are some inclinations which are fairly

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:55.029
stubborn and that they will never be entirely
defeated.

00:07:55.029 --> 00:08:01.259
And, you know, you asked, why does it matter
to what extent this is deeply biological?

00:08:01.259 --> 00:08:06.340
You know, one example is, I think for a while,
20 years ago or so, there was the idea that

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:12.919
you could have a sexual morality that was
premised on the idea that men and women are

00:08:12.919 --> 00:08:18.650
identical in the way they think about sex,
in the way they react to sex.

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:20.159
And that had a lot of effects.

00:08:20.159 --> 00:08:27.530
I actually think one of the effects it had
was to alleviate, to free males from a kind

00:08:27.530 --> 00:08:32.450
of moral responsibility they had previously
been told they had, a kind of considerateness

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:40.030
they had to extend to someone, for example,
in realizing that sex might mean less to the

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:42.820
male than to the female and they should keep
that in mind.

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:50.200
And, you know, so I think on the one hand,
if males are aware of this, they can certainly

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:53.000
— you know, we can all control ourselves.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:58.120
On the other hand, there are some basic features
of human nature that exist in cultures everywhere

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:02.351
and are reflected in the moral systems that
evolve culturally.

00:09:02.351 --> 00:09:07.720
And I think, you know, a robust moral system
has to take into account basic differences

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:10.230
that are going to be there to some extent
or another.

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:14.440
Christina Sommers: Don’t you think that
may be a mistake that some feminists made

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.870
if there are these differences between men
and women?

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:19.760
And I think a bedrock difference is that men
are stronger than women.

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:21.410
Men can harm women.

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:26.980
And there may have been a reason why we evolved
certain customs and codes of civility and

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:27.980
—

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:28.980
Ben Wattenberg: Men don’t bear children.

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:30.640
I mean, to begin with, I mean as a —

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:31.640
Christina Sommers: And men don’t bear children.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:35.350
But codes of gallantry, showing gestures of
respect towards women, those may have been

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:40.360
very, very important to protect women, not
to demean women, though there were demeaning

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:41.520
aspects, for sure.

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:48.030
But I think as feminists, perhaps we were
too hasty to disparage these customs and mores

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:51.470
that served a very important purpose of protecting
women from men.

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:52.470
Ben Wattenberg: Larry.

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:57.030
Lawrence Hedges: And yet I would be worried
if we jumped too hastily to the conclusion

00:09:57.030 --> 00:10:01.820
that the differences that we observe between
men and women are necessarily biologically

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:03.760
based and immutable.

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:09.940
That would freeze into place certain differences
in the opportunity structure for men and women

00:10:09.940 --> 00:10:12.640
in society that we might not want to freeze
into place.

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:15.720
Ben Wattenberg: Well, but I mean now you’re
talking like a politician.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:20.240
You’re supposed to be a social scientist,
which is — we do not regard that as an oxymoron

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:21.240
now.

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.150
So you shouldn’t say what should be, but
what is.

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:25.010
Now, what does your study show?

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:27.710
I mean, are there immutable differences?

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:33.220
I mean, men do not bear children — that
is a major difference in a human being’s

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:38.430
life — and don’t usually play a premier
role in rearing them and nurturing them.

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:41.520
That is another premier difference in the
lives of men and women.

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:45.100
I mean, can you then just say sort of politically,
well, we should ignore that?

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:48.980
Lawrence Hedges: I’m not suggesting we should
ignore that, but what I am suggesting is that

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:54.430
when we observe differences that are as large
as the ones we found in our study, differences

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:59.590
of two to one or seven to one in some cases,
those kinds of differences could be used to

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:06.390
suggest that it’s all right if in science
men outnumber women seven to one and it’s

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:08.470
not a difference that we should really worry
about.

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:13.250
We shouldn’t ask ourselves the question:
Could this be because there are more opportunities

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:16.310
for men and more encouragement to go into
science for men than women?

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:21.440
Deborah Tannen: There is a danger that obvious
differences — for example, women can give

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:22.440
birth.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:25.930
Some women can give birth to babies, and no
men can — obviously, not all women do —

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:26.930
Christina Sommers: Arnold Schwarzenegger —

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:28.430
Ben Wattenberg: Arnold Schwarzenegger did
in that movie, right.

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:29.430
[Laughter.]

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:31.900
Deborah Tannen: — can be misinterpreted.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:37.910
For example, one of the early arguments against
women receiving higher education was that

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:42.460
all that blood supply that was going to be
going to their brains when they did all this

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:47.980
difficult intellectual work would be taken
from the womb and would affect their childbearing

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:49.060
capacities.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:55.120
So the question is still open: What are the
impacts of these various differences?

00:11:55.120 --> 00:11:57.080
And you know, I think they can be over-interpreted.

00:11:57.080 --> 00:12:01.350
Christina Sommers: But I think the important
thing is that we must believe in equality

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:02.350
of opportunity.

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:08.990
If a little girl is a math prodigy, then classes
should be available to her, and she should

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:11.430
be able to pursue her talent.

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:16.580
Unfortunately, what’s happening right now
is many women’s groups, in particular, the

00:12:16.580 --> 00:12:21.250
American Association of University Women and
a group called Fair Test, have said, “Aha,

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:25.540
girls are not scoring the same as boys on
the SAT and on the NAEPs.

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:26.670
We have to change the test.”

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:27.670
Ben Wattenberg: The NAEPs is the —

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:29.730
Christina Sommers: The National Assessment
of Educational Progress.

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:30.730
Ben Wattenberg: Right.

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:35.470
Christina Sommers: And girls are behind in
math and science; therefore, it is sexism

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:36.470
at work.

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:42.720
And they’re actually changing SAT tests
now in ways which — to favor girls, and

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:45.870
there’s a suggestion that they should take
away time limits or they shouldn’t reward

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:46.900
boys for guessing.

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:48.230
Now, this is ridiculous.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:52.840
It’s part of intelligence to make educated
guesses and to be quick-witted.

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:55.530
I mean, it just seems to me to be all wrong.

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:57.300
We’re going — it’s the dumbing down.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:00.840
Lawrence Hedges: It seems to me that it’s
a very dangerous phenomenon because it leads

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:05.330
to the idea of redefining the criteria, and
not just for men and women, but for any groups

00:13:05.330 --> 00:13:07.190
that happen to be important in the society.

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:10.610
And I think that’s a serious mistake of
shooting the messenger rather than trying

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:12.150
to find out what we can learn from the message.

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:17.150
Robert Wright: You know, I wanted to say that
— you wanted to be kind of agnostic on the

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:19.520
basis of cognitive differences between men
and women.

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:22.811
And it’s actually true that from the point
of view of evolutionary psychology; there

00:13:22.811 --> 00:13:27.930
aren’t really clear reasons to predict very
specific cognitive differences.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:30.260
That’s not to say they don’t have an evolutionary
basis.

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:37.960
But what is fairly likely is that in the realm
of what you might call ambition, there is

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:40.170
some difference between the average man and
the average woman.

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:41.840
And that’s the kind of thing that could
account —

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:42.840
Ben Wattenberg: Well, what is the difference?

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:43.840
Who is more ambitious?

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:44.840
Robert Wright: Oh, men.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:50.580
And in Deborah’s work, you — I mean, men
are bigger show-offs, and there’s a fairly

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:54.620
clear-cut evolutionary reason for this.

00:13:54.620 --> 00:14:00.360
And another related thing from an evolutionary
standpoint is there’s some reason to believe

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:07.060
that parents may have an innate inclination
to push boys harder because during evolution,

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:13.130
the social status of a son paid off in reproductive
terms more than the social status of a daughter.

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:17.130
Now, that doesn’t mean that parents are
condemned to do this for eternity.

00:14:17.130 --> 00:14:22.730
In fact, in my own experience — I have two
daughters — being aware of this tendency,

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:28.030
if anything, makes me actually want to combat
it, I mean, you know, give the daughter a

00:14:28.030 --> 00:14:29.400
fair shake.

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:34.330
And to the extent that parents do that, then
any difference in your findings, for example,

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:36.440
that’s due to parents pushing the male will
disappear.

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:42.370
Ben Wattenberg: Is it possible that men and
women are equally ambitious, but for different

00:14:42.370 --> 00:14:44.030
goals?

00:14:44.030 --> 00:14:45.030
That a man may want to be —

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:46.030
Robert Wright: Well, in different realms.

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:49.480
Ben Wattenberg: — the football star or the
CEO, and a woman maybe extremely ambitious

00:14:49.480 --> 00:14:56.160
— I mean, to go back an era or so — may
be extremely ambitious to raise three children

00:14:56.160 --> 00:15:02.290
really well and have a home and a family and
do things that, you know, are, in my judgment,

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:05.110
more important than about 99 percent of the
other kind of jobs.

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:06.110
But that’s —

00:15:06.110 --> 00:15:10.060
Robert Wright: Well, and do things that are
even less obviously related to kind of being

00:15:10.060 --> 00:15:11.370
a mother.

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:17.220
But certainly, yes, my guess would be, based
on evolutionary psychology, that in the realm

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:24.200
of kind of being — you know, being out there
and seeking respect — another difference

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:28.390
you find is that men want respect and women
want women to — want people to like them,

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:29.390
at least on average.

00:15:29.390 --> 00:15:31.200
Deborah Tannen: In terms of percentages and
degree.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:32.200
Robert Wright: Exactly.

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:33.780
I mean, all of these are statistical differences.

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:39.280
But in that realm, you would expect a difference
in that direction.

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:41.090
Christina Sommers: You know what I find surprising,
though?

00:15:41.090 --> 00:15:46.740
If you look at Department of Education data,
more girls are going to college than boys.

00:15:46.740 --> 00:15:49.150
And they ask, “Were you encouraged to go?”

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:53.240
They ask counselors, “Were you encouraged
by a teacher, a counselor, a parent?”

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:56.210
And far more girls claim to have been encouraged.

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:59.080
So there may be something amiss in our society
right now.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:03.170
I’m a mother of two boys, so I’m the boys’
advocate here.

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:09.370
But I really feel that we are not encouraging
our boys as much as our girls now, that boys

00:16:09.370 --> 00:16:10.370
are getting left behind.

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:14.600
And there is, I know, a great deal of hype
about the shortchanged, silenced girls.

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:19.540
But if you actually look who’s getting the
better grades, it’s the girls, who is in

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:23.141
most of the honors clubs and the service clubs
and the school newspaper, it’s girls who

00:16:23.141 --> 00:16:24.141
are overrepresented.

00:16:24.141 --> 00:16:28.500
The one area where boys are overrepresented
is sports, and the feminists are going after

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:29.630
that with a vengeance.

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:33.181
Ben Wattenberg: Who gets in more trouble with
drugs and crime?

00:16:33.181 --> 00:16:37.650
Christina Sommers: Drugs and alcohol and delinquency,
dropping out — boys are overrepresented

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:38.650
in those areas.

00:16:38.650 --> 00:16:42.320
Lawrence Hedges: But the finding that women
— that girls get better grades than boys

00:16:42.320 --> 00:16:43.440
is an old finding.

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:48.940
It goes way back to a time I think before
the feminist revolution and has persisted

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:50.820
to this day in every area.

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:52.320
Christina Sommers: Better study habits.

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:54.390
Lawrence Hedges: Or greater task persistence.

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:55.390
Who knows?

00:16:55.390 --> 00:16:58.040
Ben Wattenberg: Or an innate difference.

00:16:58.040 --> 00:16:59.910
Robert Wright: Could be also possible.

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:04.180
Deborah Tannen: Well, it doesn’t — I mean,
you poo-poo all the findings of the — why

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:06.500
girls fail stuff, and sure, you can —

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:07.500
Christina Sommers: Not all of them, not all
of them.

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:10.669
Deborah Tannen: — because you can pick out
lots of them that I think have been misinterpreted,

00:17:10.669 --> 00:17:12.340
but there’s a lot of stuff there, I think,
that’s very real.

00:17:12.340 --> 00:17:13.340
Ben Wattenberg: For example?

00:17:13.340 --> 00:17:14.340
Christina Sommers: Like what?

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:17.560
Deborah Tannen: Well, for example, the findings
that girls are called on less in class, that

00:17:17.560 --> 00:17:18.560
they —

00:17:18.560 --> 00:17:19.560
Christina Sommers: No, no.

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:22.300
Look at the latest research, Jerry Brofy at
Michigan State and Jacqueline Eckles.

00:17:22.300 --> 00:17:23.500
It’s really a myth.

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:24.520
Ben Wattenberg: What do —

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:26.770
Deborah Tannen: Well, I’ve been convinced
by the research I’ve read —

00:17:26.770 --> 00:17:27.770
Ben Wattenberg: Hold it.

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:31.260
Let’s try — what do they say, and then
you tell me, what is the counterargument on

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:32.260
this?

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:35.280
Christina Sommers: Jerry Brofy at Michigan
State says that the — he simply — boys

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:38.090
are — get more attention because it’s
negative attention.

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:39.640
They are disciplined more.

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:42.890
And if you’re sitting there observing, you
will see, “Johnny, what do you think is

00:17:42.890 --> 00:17:44.630
the capital of New York?”

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:47.440
It’s a form of classroom management.

00:17:47.440 --> 00:17:51.740
So boys get — but what’s important, according
to Brofy and others, is the quality of the

00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:52.740
interaction.

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:55.880
And there, boys are not getting more qualitative
interaction.

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:57.450
Ben Wattenberg: Deborah Tannen, what do you
think about that?

00:17:57.450 --> 00:18:00.730
Deborah Tannen: Well, the research findings
of the Satgers, I’m sure you all know them

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:01.730
—

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:02.730
Christina Sommers: Where did the Satgers publish
that data?

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:04.260
Deborah Tannen: They’ve done — in their
— their book called “Failing at Fairness”

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:05.260
is full of this —

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:06.710
Christina Sommers: No, no, but what research
journal?

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:08.260
Where has their data ever been peer-reviewed?

00:18:08.260 --> 00:18:09.260
It hasn’t.

00:18:09.260 --> 00:18:12.240
Deborah Tannen: I’ve read their research
and many others, too.

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:14.710
Christina Sommers: That’s what everyone
thinks, that there are many others, but they’re

00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:15.710
not there.

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:16.920
Deborah Tannen: Well, I believe they’re
there.

00:18:16.920 --> 00:18:18.350
I mean, it’s not my immediate field.

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:19.350
Ben Wattenberg: Okay, all right.

00:18:19.350 --> 00:18:22.400
Deborah Tannen: But let me just say, the kind
of thing that they find is that if a boy asks

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:27.110
a question, the teacher is more likely to
ask him a challenging question, more likely

00:18:27.110 --> 00:18:29.331
to follow up with questions that challenge
him.

00:18:29.331 --> 00:18:30.331
Ben Wattenberg: But let me ask you —

00:18:30.331 --> 00:18:32.870
Deborah Tannen: And I observe this in my own
classrooms, girls in my — women — young

00:18:32.870 --> 00:18:34.420
women in my own classrooms.

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:36.731
I have classes of women and men.

00:18:36.731 --> 00:18:38.480
I’ve done my own studies on this, too.

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:43.080
I will often have three, four, five young
women in the classroom never speak in class.

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:47.520
I very rarely have young men in the class
that never speak in class.

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:52.250
Women tell me that if they speak in class
one day, they try to be quiet the next because

00:18:52.250 --> 00:18:53.250
they don’t want to be seen as dominating.

00:18:53.250 --> 00:18:57.071
Ben Wattenberg: In the elementary and secondary
school, at least, the vast majority of the

00:18:57.071 --> 00:18:59.070
teachers are women.

00:18:59.070 --> 00:19:05.200
So if there is a discriminatory act going
on, it is women on women, right?

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:09.080
Deborah Tannen: Well, that’s very interesting
because I think definitely a lot of the expectations

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:13.840
that we have, which we foist on others — in
other words, you expect a woman to be a certain

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:17.740
way, you expect a man to be a certain way
— it’s equally true of women as it is

00:19:17.740 --> 00:19:18.740
of men.

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:19.740
There’s no reason that it shouldn’t be.

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:24.270
Ben Wattenberg: Let me ask you a question
as a social scientist.

00:19:24.270 --> 00:19:31.030
How much of the social science in America
today is pre-driven, that in other words,

00:19:31.030 --> 00:19:35.550
somebody is going in there with a liberal
view or a conservative view and saying, “Here’s

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:36.711
what I’m looking to prove”?

00:19:36.711 --> 00:19:39.420
And we all know you can jiggle a lot of these
studies.

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:43.520
And how much of it is the equivalent of a
man or a woman in a white coat in a medical

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:46.130
lab, you know, checking everything one against
another?

00:19:46.130 --> 00:19:48.320
Are the social sciences ideologically driven?

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:51.980
Lawrence Hedges: To some extent, of course
they are.

00:19:51.980 --> 00:19:56.910
And they have to be because, you know, it
is social science.

00:19:56.910 --> 00:20:03.220
But many of the large data-collection programs
that are multipurpose data-collection programs

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:08.690
are so large, involve so many different scientists,
and have to be vetted by so many different

00:20:08.690 --> 00:20:13.650
interests that in fact I suspect many of those
special interests and special perspectives

00:20:13.650 --> 00:20:15.160
kind of cancel one another out.

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:20.770
Ben Wattenberg: Insofar as what we’ve been
talking about, what should we do about education?

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:24.070
Let’s go — starting with you, Bob Wright,
and come around this way.

00:20:24.070 --> 00:20:28.140
Robert Wright: Well, I mean, the policy prescription
that’s clearest to me and has some relevance

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:34.840
to education is that if affirmative action
for the sexes is to be based on the assumption

00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:40.811
that the sexes are identical in nature, so
that any imbalance in the representation of

00:20:40.811 --> 00:20:46.900
males and females in a given workforce or
at a given level is assumed to be due to discrimination,

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:48.340
that is surely a faulty assumption.

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:51.410
I really think, from the point of evolutionary
psychology, you can say that.

00:20:51.410 --> 00:20:57.770
On the other hand, evolutionary psychology
provides you reasons why an enlightened boss

00:20:57.770 --> 00:21:02.120
might, in some cases, in a sense systematically
favor women over men.

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:09.760
It suggests that men are more likely to be
egotistical in their pursuit of career advancement

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:14.150
and even sacrifice the interests of the organization
they’re working for to their own personal

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:16.010
interests than women are.

00:21:16.010 --> 00:21:19.320
This is not a hundred percent certain finding,
but there’s very good reason to believe

00:21:19.320 --> 00:21:20.540
it.

00:21:20.540 --> 00:21:25.270
So I think, you know, in policy terms, this
can in some senses cut against women, in some

00:21:25.270 --> 00:21:27.820
senses favor them.

00:21:27.820 --> 00:21:32.100
Ben Wattenberg: Larry, Bob Wright has expanded
the topic from education to education and

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:33.100
affirmative action.

00:21:33.100 --> 00:21:36.440
Now, where would you — what should we be
doing that we’re not doing?

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:41.840
Lawrence Hedges: Given that we don’t know
that the differences are entirely biological,

00:21:41.840 --> 00:21:46.810
I think we should try to do more to equalize
opportunity structures for men and women in

00:21:46.810 --> 00:21:49.011
education, first of all, in the long run.

00:21:49.011 --> 00:21:52.670
Ben Wattenberg: And that would mean, for example,
working harder with boys to become better

00:21:52.670 --> 00:21:53.670
writers.

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:55.040
Lawrence Hedges: As one of the prime examples.

00:21:55.040 --> 00:21:58.020
Ben Wattenberg: Right, as well as girls to
become better mathematicians.

00:21:58.020 --> 00:21:59.020
Lawrence Hedges: Exactly.

00:21:59.020 --> 00:22:02.360
But in the — I would like to add that in
the short run, the imbalance of very talented

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:07.450
individuals — that is, the fact that there
are more very talented men than women in areas

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:13.380
like mathematics and science — has real
implications for trying to achieve closer

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:16.730
to a balance of representation of the genders
in the sciences.

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:21.920
It means that we either have to accept lower
goals or recruit harder to get more women

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:22.920
into the sciences.

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:23.920
Ben Wattenberg: Deborah Tannen.

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:28.550
Deborah Tannen: I agree with what Bob Wright
said earlier, that if there are differences,

00:22:28.550 --> 00:22:33.680
it’s in everybody’s interest to understand
what they are and move on from there.

00:22:33.680 --> 00:22:39.570
I think that for work situations as well as
classroom situations and home relationships,

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:44.059
if we can see patterns and identify them,
we can equalize opportunity by taking them

00:22:44.059 --> 00:22:45.059
into account.

00:22:45.059 --> 00:22:50.059
An example that has come up is very good evidence
that men are more likely than women to be

00:22:50.059 --> 00:22:55.250
aggressive and to use open argumentation,
and this could also be cultural because it

00:22:55.250 --> 00:22:57.570
may not be true of Japanese and other cultures.

00:22:57.570 --> 00:23:04.720
But in the Western society, the open exchange
of argument as a way of learning things is

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:07.620
much more congenial to the men than the women.

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:11.590
And this could be one reason that many women
back off in a classroom that is taught that

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:12.590
way.

00:23:12.590 --> 00:23:16.240
And there’s a fair amount of evidence that
women often drop out of science classes because

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:21.790
they don’t like that argumentative classroom
atmosphere and they’re uncomfortable with

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:22.790
it.

00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:26.710
Well, that’s a way you could start out,
not lowering the requirements for how much

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:31.550
a scientist has to know to get this job, by
no means, but creating an environment that

00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:35.020
women might be more comfortable getting the
scientific training and might not drop out.

00:23:35.020 --> 00:23:36.020
Ben Wattenberg: Christina.

00:23:36.020 --> 00:23:40.870
Christina Sommers: I think one thing that’s
very important is that a lot of the research

00:23:40.870 --> 00:23:46.390
on gender in the last 10 or 15 years has been
done by feminist advocates who are committed

00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:50.300
to a sort of victim feminism, and the research
cannot be trusted.

00:23:50.300 --> 00:23:54.910
They exaggerate women’s vulnerability; they
exaggerate women’s oppression.

00:23:54.910 --> 00:24:01.100
And I hope that politicians and journalists
will be careful to get to the more balanced

00:24:01.100 --> 00:24:05.250
scholars because I think in education we’re
not getting a good, accurate picture.

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:08.580
I think when it comes to gender bias in the
workplace, we’re not getting an accurate

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:09.580
picture.

00:24:09.580 --> 00:24:12.770
We have to get to the — as I said, to the
more solid research.

00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:14.160
Ben Wattenberg: Okay.

00:24:14.160 --> 00:24:20.520
Thank you, Christina Sommers, Lawrence Hedges,
Robert Wright, and Deborah Tannen.

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:21.380
Thank you.

